<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lugalbanda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lugalbanda was a legendary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, said to be the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> and the husband of the goddess Ninsun. Two Sumerian epic poems tell of his adventures in the mountains while campaigning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratta">Aratta</a>, including his encounter with the Anzu bird. He was later worshipped as a god at Uruk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugalbanda.jpg" alt="Lugalbanda" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Obverse._The_story_of_Lugalbanda_in_the_Mountain_Cave,_Old-Babylonian_period,_from_southern_Iraq._Sulaymaniyah_Museum,_Iraqi_Kurdistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
